  "id": "h12",
  "order": 12,
  "title": "Broken chords in the left hand",
  "summary": "Play a chord's notes one after another, quickly and lightly, like Für Elise.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "type": "explain",
      "title": "One chord, one note at a time",
      "body": "In Für Elise the left hand doesn't press its chords all at once. It plays them low to high: little finger, pointing finger, thumb.\n\nA chord played one note after another is a **broken chord**.\n\n**Why it matters:** a quick left hand comes from a hand that stays in one shape while the fingers take turns. If the hand moves for every note, it can't go fast.\n\nThese are Für Elise's two chords, A minor and E major, a little higher than in the piece so they fit your keyboard. Press **Watch**.",
